From 15caf5755fc3fc3312d545b6e8c7df959c9cab72 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gx-bangsong <146644201+gx-bangsong@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 05:23:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] numberinfo: enforce lookup time budgets on blocking
 network I/O

The per-source (800ms) and total (3.5s) screening budgets were applied
with withTimeoutOrNull around HttpURLConnection calls. Blocking socket
I/O ignores coroutine cancellation, so a stalled source held a lookup
for its full connect/read timeouts (3s + 3s per rules.json) and the
late result was still used. Measured against a black-hole server: 3.0s
for an 800ms budget. Telecom gives the screening service ~5s.

- HttpFetcher.fetch() is now suspending and cancellable: the I/O runs on
  an executor, cancellation returns immediately and disconnects the
  connection (aborting a blocked connect/read); the body read is also
  capped at 2x timeoutMs. fetchBlocking() keeps the old behaviour for
  RuleRepository.
- awaitWithin() bounds the wait rather than the work; ProviderManager
  uses it per source (now 0.8s against the same server).
- DialerCallScreeningService answers Telecom within TOTAL_BUDGET_MS no
  matter what, responds before persisting the blocked-call record, and
  fails open if setup throws.
